ound 91 degrees with little veer."}, {"before_call": 15, "text": "I'm adjusting the test parameters and trying a few variants. Test 4 came out about 4 too far east, and I'm wondering whether a stronger updraft would help or hurt, since a longer fall time could add eastward drift while drag slows things down, so the effect is unclear."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "I'm comparing the Test 5 drag prediction against the observed westward travel, and the sign of the discrepancy suggests either more drag than modeled or a wind effect. I'm also moving on to Test 6, where dt is -0.12.\n\nI'm checking the Test 6 drag prediction against the observed travel, where the predicted distance falls short at 30m, so more drag looks likely. I'm trying drag 0.04 with the wind and shear values, but the north-heading runs, which wanted about 0.037, conflict with the west-heading run needing more, so I'm wondering whether wind direction or veer is involved. Then I'll just try drag 0.039 and see."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "The drag at 0.037 looks right on fall time, since the observed times of 10.81 and 10.96 seconds at 150 m fit it. Test 6 at 30 m came out a bit shorter than predicted, though, and the dt figures have been drifting the wrong way.\n\nI'm checking the residuals left over from the current drag fit: test 5 is off by about 4.6 m in x and 1.5 m in y, and test 2 by 2.7 and 2.0. The y misses are largest on the high drops, and the observed y for test 5 sits above prediction, which would point to a small northward wind aloft and a slight negative veer. That effect looks too small to explain it on its own, so I'll use the remaining high-altitude drops to pin down the wind parameters.\n\nI'm also weighing what matters for operations, since the altitude ranges of the targets aren't known. A full least-squares fit by hand is impractical, so I'll try a few parameter variations instead: wind speed 7.2, shear 0.3, veer -3, and direction 91."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "The results look mostly within noise, with test 4 showing a notable +3.7 in dx that I'll attribute to updraft uncertainty. I'm going to refine the updraft with more smoke drops, and I'll try values of 3.5 and 4.5 to see how it responds."}, {"before_call": 19, "text": "I'm weighing the updraft value: the dt readings at 4 and 3.5 bracket zero near 3.9, so I'll take about 3.9. The +3 drift in test 4 still bothers me, since a 60 m drop heading east at speed 45 could be more drag or lower wind near the ground, and I'm checking the wind prediction at 60 m.\n\nI'm checking that the wind at 60 m fits the measured value, and it does within about 1.5 m of noise. For the remaining four drops I'm planning to use smoke at higher altitude, where updraft drift is largest, and I'm running a simulation of a release at x=-50, altitude 120, heading 90, speed 50 to design the test."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "I'm working out the travel distance: 120 m, starting from the -50 edge and ending at 69, so the path crosses the smoke over a 100 m stretch. That's decent, but I'd only be inside the smoke part of the time, so I'm considering a lower speed to stay in it longer. The minimum speed is 45, so I'm settling on releasing at x=-55."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "I'm picking a new test setup for the smoke case: altitude 30, speed 45, with the smoke at the center. I'm weighing a few starting positions and headings, and checking whether the travel distance and a roughly 3.5 second fall time fit. Some of the targets might need a different approach as well.\n\nI'm still weighing the alternate configurations, since the unknown targets might need a different setup and I'm okay with that for now."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "I'm checking the shear values at low altitudes: test 6 is off by -0.12 and test 3 by +0.08, which I'm treating as acceptable. There seems to be a systematic -1 in dy, so I'm considering nudging the wind direction slightly more northward, to 90.5 with a veer of -3, and I'll try that."}, {"before_call": 24, "text": "I'm going with this approach now. I'm weighing whether to run the remaining test drops, but I think skipping them is fine, so I'm starting the operation."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "I'm planning to start from central values and run the simulation with the release point set to the target, then shift the release by the gap between target and impact. I'll iterate once or twice, since the updraft effect depends on the smoke path, which itself depends on the release. For the first target I have heading 84, altitude 55, and speed 67, with the release set at the target to begin."}, {"before_call": 30, "text": "I'm shifting the release point by the negative of the miss, since the updraft depends on the path and may need another pass. Trying the first offset gives a new release near (1028, -299), and I'm running the same adjustment on the next few trial cases.\n\nChecking the remaining trial offsets, T4 and T5, to see where the release points land with the shift applied. The load enters the smoke at different spots once the release moves, so I'll need to re-simulate."}, {"before_call": 35, "text": "I'm checking the T3 correction first: it's off by (2.5, 0), so the release x moves to 217.5. T4 comes out to (+1.8, -0.8), which puts it at (-1519.5, -1222.1). Both adjustments are small, so I'll apply them. The robustness question about the uncertain updraft doesn't change anything here, since the high-altitude smoke interactions are fine as they are, so I'm dropping that concern."}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 278848, "completion_tokens": 11824, "cache_read_tokens": 261701, "requests": 25}, "stopped": "done"}, "agent": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "run": 3}